      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "This study sought to define the activity and toxicity of weekly docetaxel in patients with androgen-independent prostate cancer and cancer-related pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty-five patients were treated with docetaxel 36 mg/m2 i.v. administered weekly for six consecutive weeks followed by two weeks without treatment. This eight-week treatment cycle was repeated until progression or unacceptable toxicity. Endpoints included palliative response (a 2-point reduction on the 6-point Present Pain Intensity scale without an increase in analgesic consumption or a 50% decrease in analgesic use without an increase in pain), PSA response (a 50% decrease maintained at least four weeks), measurable disease response, survival, and toxicity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Twelve of 25 patients (48%, 95% confidence interval (95% CI): 28%-68%) had a palliative response. Eleven of the 24 patients who entered with an elevated PSA (46%, 95% CI: 25%-67%) had a PSA response. Two of five patients with measurable disease had a partial response. Toxicity of therapy was modest with no treatment-related mortality. Twenty-five percent of patients experienced a grade 3 or 4 hematologic toxicity and 36% of patients experienced a grade 3 non-hematologic toxicity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Weekly docetaxel is well tolerated in patients with androgen-independent prostate cancer and has significant activity as measured by relief of pain, reduction in PSA, and reduction in measurable disease."
        }
      ]},
